course: dictionary
lesson: 11460
title: Admixture: a small amount mixed in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up and serious. Admixture is one of them. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in science books and careful writing rather than in a chat with friends. «The bricks were made with a small admixture of sand.» The words around it give clues: something (sand) was added to something else (the bricks) and mixed in.
explain: Look at the clue words. «A small admixture» tells you the amount is little, and «of sand» tells you what was added. So an admixture is something added and mixed in, usually a small amount. In everyday talk you could say "a bit of" or "a mix" instead.
explain: Admixture can also mean the act of mixing things in. «The admixture of the two liquids took only a minute.» Here the word is about the mixing itself, not the thing added. Ask yourself: does the sentence talk about the added thing, or about the mixing? The rest of the sentence tells you which.
explain: In science books about families and ancestry, admixture means the mixing of different groups of people over time, for example when people from different groups have children together. Read the whole passage to see which meaning fits. When in doubt, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Admixture is a formal word, so it is used in serious writing and science.
fact: «Admixture» means something added to something else and mixed in, especially a small amount.
fact: In everyday speech you could say «a bit of», «mix» or «mixture» instead.
fact: Admixture can also mean the act of mixing something in.
fact: In science about genes and ancestry, admixture means the mixing of different family lines (populations) over time.
exercise: «The bricks were made with a small admixture of sand.» What does admixture mean in this sentence? | A hard, cold wall | The place where bricks are sold | A large empty space | *Something small added and mixed in
exercise: Which everyday phrase could replace admixture in «a small admixture of salt in the water»? | A big hole | *A bit of | Nothing at all | Hot and cold
exercise: Why would a science writer use admixture instead of mix? | It means the same as sleep | It is shorter | *It is a formal, technical word that suits serious writing | It is a joke word for children
test: In a history book: «The admixture of different peoples changed the village over many years.» What does admixture mean here? | A large wooden door | *The mixing of different groups of people over time | Empty space in a village | A quick walk
test: Which sentence uses admixture correctly? | *The sauce had a small admixture of pepper. | The sauce was admixture hungry. | The admixture ran away and hid. | He admixture the dog to the park.
exam: «The lake water had an admixture of mud, so it looked brown.» What does admixture mean here? | A deep blue colour | A loud splash | *A small amount of mud mixed in | A cold wind
